# plan spec

---
intent: >
  Re-align the existing `npx aidemd-mcp sync` verb to the post-collapse
  brain.aide schema and path. Sync now reads `.aide/config/brain.aide`
  (was `.aide/brain.aide`); takes the parsed `mcpServerConfig` as its
  derived MCP entry (no `rootPath`/`entryFile`-specific handling); surfaces
  the new path in every error string. The verb's contract — read brain.aide,
  derive entry, write `.mcp.json` under the fixed `brain` key, idempotent,
  legacy `obsidian` key removal in the same write — is unchanged in shape.
  The shared `writeMcpEntry` helper at `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/`
  needs no edits; it already takes an entries map and works against the
  collapsed schema unchanged.
---

## Project Structure

This is an additive/edit plan against an existing module. The folder
shape is unchanged from the prior `plan.aide`; only the contents of
`index.ts` and `index.test.ts` shift to track the post-collapse contract.

```
src/cli/sync/
├── .aide                        ← [existing — already aligned for the new path/schema]
├── plan.aide                    ← [this file — rewritten]
├── todo.aide                    ← [existing — every drift item resolved]
├── index.ts                     ← [edit] CLI entry point
│                                   exports:
│                                     export async function runSync(
│                                       cwd: string,
│                                       write?: (line: string) => void,
│                                       writeErr?: (line: string) => void,
│                                     ): Promise<number>
│                                   IIFE at file bottom dispatches argv:
│                                     --help → usage prose, exit 2
│                                     <no flags> → call runSync(process.cwd()),
│                                                  exit with returned code
│                                   The IIFE's --help text and runSync's stderr
│                                   strings ALL name `.aide/config/brain.aide`
│                                   (was `.aide/brain.aide`). The pipeline body
│                                   is otherwise unchanged: parse → derive entry
│                                   → call shared writeMcpEntry → report.
└── index.test.ts                ← [edit] co-located tests; every fixture
                                   rewrites to the two-field schema (`name` +
                                   `mcpServerConfig`), uses `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
                                   as the launcher, embeds the path inline in
                                   args (no `${rootPath}` placeholder), and
                                   writes brain.aide to `.aide/config/brain.aide`.
                                   Test 3j is replaced (the default scaffold no
                                   longer exercises interpolation). Legacy
                                   `obsidian` migration test is preserved
                                   unchanged in intent — the migration contract
                                   is unchanged.
```

This plan does NOT touch any of the following — they are already correct
or owned by other plans:

- `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` — the shared helper takes an
  entries map and is schema-agnostic. The change does not reach it.
- `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/index.test.ts` — same; helper tests
  are independent of brain.aide schema.
- `src/types/index.ts` — `BrainAideConfig` collapses inside the
  parseBrainAide plan (Plan 2 in that module). This plan consumes the
  collapsed type but does not edit it.

## Prerequisites

This plan depends on the post-collapse contract of `parseBrainAide`. The
following must be true before this plan's steps execute:

**`src/service/parseBrainAide/`** (Plan 2 in that module — see
`src/service/parseBrainAide/plan.aide`) must export the post-collapse
contract:

- `parseBrainAide(root: string)` reads from `join(root, ".aide", "config",
  "brain.aide")` (was `.aide/brain.aide`). Returns the same
  `ParseBrainAideResult` discriminated union: `ok | missing |
  malformed-frontmatter | malformed-body`.
- The `ok` variant carries a `config: BrainAideConfig` shape that is
  exactly `{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command: string; args:
  string[] } }` — no `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, or `tools`
  fields.
- `interpolateArgs(config)` continues to exist and walks any
  `${<key>}` placeholder against top-level frontmatter fields; with
  the collapsed schema the only resolvable placeholder is `${name}`,
  and the default scaffold contains no placeholders so the helper is
  a no-op for default installs. Sync calls it unchanged.
- The `malformed-frontmatter` variant's `reason` string names every
  deprecated field encountered (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
  `tools`) when present, so sync can pass the reason through verbatim
  and the user sees exactly which stale fields to remove.

**`src/types/index.ts`** must export the collapsed `BrainAideConfig` and
`BrainAideMcpServerConfig` types. This plan reads those types via
`ParseBrainAideResult` from the parser module — it does not import them
directly, but the type narrowing through `result.config.mcpServerConfig`
relies on the collapse having landed.

If parseBrainAide is still on the legacy schema when this plan runs,
the test fixtures in step 2 will fail to parse (deprecated-field
rejection) and step 1's `expectedEntry` derivation will type-error on
the missing `BrainAideConfig` fields. Run parseBrainAide's plan first.

## Plan

### 1. Edit error strings and pipeline references in `runSync`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/agentic/command-modules`

- [x] Open `src/cli/sync/index.ts`. Replace every literal occurrence
      of `` `.aide/brain.aide` `` (in stderr lines, the `--help` usage
      prose, and any inline comments) with `` `.aide/config/brain.aide` ``.
      The strings to update appear in:

      - The `result.kind === "missing"` stderr line ("No
        `.aide/config/brain.aide` found. Run `npx aidemd-mcp init`
        to scaffold it, or create the file by hand.").
      - The `result.kind === "malformed-frontmatter"` stderr line
        ("`.aide/config/brain.aide` frontmatter is malformed:
        `<reason>`. Fix the YAML and re-run sync.").
      - The `result.kind === "malformed-body"` stderr line
        ("`.aide/config/brain.aide` is missing the `## Prose` section:
        `<reason>`. Fix the file and re-run sync.").
      - The success stdout line preceding the entry write
        ("Read .aide/config/brain.aide").
      - The IIFE's `--help` usage prose paragraph that names the
        conventional brain.aide path
        (`brain.aide  →  <cwd>/.aide/config/brain.aide`).
      - Any inline comments that reference the old path (e.g. step
        comments narrating the parseBrainAide pipeline).

      The malformed-frontmatter line continues to interpolate the
      parser's `result.reason` byte-for-byte. Per the spec
      `outcomes.desired[0]`, the parser names every deprecated field
      it found in the reason; sync passes that reason through
      verbatim. No paraphrase, no wrapping that would obscure which
      stale fields the user must remove.

- [x] Confirm the `expectedEntry` derivation in `runSync` reads
      ONLY from `result.config.mcpServerConfig`:

      ```
      expectedEntry = {
        command: result.config.mcpServerConfig.command,
        args: interpolateArgs(result.config),
      }
      ```

      No reads of `result.config.rootPath`, `result.config.entryFile`,
      `result.config.connector`, or `result.config.tools` — those
      fields are removed from the collapsed `BrainAideConfig`. The
      current code already matches this shape (it only touches
      `mcpServerConfig.command` and feeds the full config to
      `interpolateArgs`); this step's job is to verify that and fix
      the moment if any leftover reference appears.

- [x] The shared `writeMcpEntry(cwd, entries)` call site is unchanged.
      The entries map continues to set `brain: expectedEntry` and
      always include `obsidian: "delete"` (the helper treats
      "delete a key that isn't present" as a no-op). This is the
      load-bearing migration surface and stays exactly as today.

- [x] The malformed-JSON catch (the `try/catch` around `writeMcpEntry`
      that translates the helper's invalid-JSON throw to an
      action-naming stderr line) is unchanged. The helper's error
      message has not moved.

- [x] Confirm `runSync`'s exit-code contract is preserved:
      - 0 on success (write OR no-change — "already in sync" is
        success).
      - 1 on operational failure (missing brain.aide,
        malformed-frontmatter, malformed-body, malformed `.mcp.json`,
        any uncaught throw at the IIFE boundary).
      - 2 on `--help` (handled by the IIFE before `runSync` is
        called).

      Per the spec `outcomes.desired[5]` and the Strategy paragraph
      "Exit codes follow Unix convention," any change to this map is
      a regression. Step 1 only edits strings; the control flow
      stays.

### 2. Update test fixtures to the two-field schema and new path

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Open `src/cli/sync/index.test.ts`. The current `VALID_BRAIN_AIDE`
      fixture renders the legacy five-field schema (`connector`,
      `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `mcpServerConfig` with `obsidian-mcp`,
      `tools.read`/`tools.search`). The new fixture must collapse to
      the two-field shape that the post-collapse parser accepts. Use
      the canonical Good-example bytes from the parent spec
      (`src/cli/sync/.aide` → "A user retargets their vault and runs
      sync"; mirrored in `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` § Format).

      The new fixture shape (illustrative — the exact bytes are the
      test author's; only field shape matters):

      ```
      ---
      name: obsidian
      mcpServerConfig:
        command: npx
        args:
          - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
          - "<TEST_VAULT_PATH>"
      ---

      ## Prose

      <hand-written prose body — used as-is by tests verifying
      no-prose-mutation; can be the canonical fixture from the
      brain-aide.md doc>
      ```

      Update the corresponding `EXPECTED_BRAIN_ENTRY` constant to
      match the collapsed args shape (no `-y`, no `obsidian-mcp`;
      `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` plus the path inline byte-for-byte).
      The `-y` flag is gone — sync writes whichever args the user
      authored in their `brain.aide`, and the canonical default
      uses `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` directly.

- [x] Update the `writeBrainAide` helper's filesystem layout so it
      writes to `.aide/config/brain.aide` instead of `.aide/brain.aide`:

      The helper currently does:
      `await mkdir(join(root, ".aide"), { recursive: true });`
      `await writeFile(join(root, ".aide", "brain.aide"), ...);`

      Update to:
      `await mkdir(join(root, ".aide", "config"), { recursive: true });`
      `await writeFile(join(root, ".aide", "config", "brain.aide"), ...);`

      Every test that pre-writes a brain.aide via this helper now
      lands the file at the new path. The path move is end-to-end:
      the parser reads from the new path, the test setup writes to
      the new path.

- [x] Update the malformed-fixture string literals in tests 3f
      (malformed frontmatter) and 3g (missing prose body) to use the
      two-field schema as their starting point:

      - 3f's `badFrontmatter` should start from the collapsed shape
        (`name: obsidian` + `mcpServerConfig:` block) and corrupt
        ONE thing — e.g. an unclosed bracket inside `args`. The
        legacy `connector: obsidian` / `rootPath: ...` / `tools:`
        lines must be removed; the parser will reject those as
        deprecated even when the YAML is otherwise valid, and the
        test would erroneously pass for the wrong reason.
      - 3g's `noProseBody` should also use the collapsed shape and
        omit only the `## Prose` heading. Same rationale.

- [x] Update test 3k (no prose interpolation) to use the collapsed
      schema. The prose body keeps its `${rootPath}` and `${entryFile}`
      literal characters — the test's load-bearing assertion is that
      the prose body is byte-unchanged after sync, which is unaffected
      by the schema collapse. The placeholders in the prose body
      remain literal characters; the parser does not touch the prose
      body, and `interpolateArgs` does not see the prose body. Even
      though `${rootPath}` and `${entryFile}` are no longer
      resolvable frontmatter fields, leaving them in the prose body
      asserts the no-prose-mutation rule against placeholder strings
      that COULD have been resolvable — a stronger test than using
      arbitrary placeholders.

### 3. Replace test 3j (args interpolation) with a default-scaffold no-op assertion

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] Test 3j currently exercises `interpolateArgs` by writing a
      brain.aide with a `${rootPath}` placeholder in args and asserting
      the literal `rootPath` value gets substituted into the
      `.mcp.json` brain entry. Under the collapsed schema, the default
      scaffold makes no use of substitution (the path lives byte-inline
      in args), and the only resolvable placeholder is `${name}`.
      Replace 3j with a test that asserts the default-scaffold args
      pass through `interpolateArgs` unchanged:

      Test name: "default-scaffold args pass through verbatim
      (interpolateArgs is a no-op)"

      Setup: pre-write a brain.aide with the canonical two-field
      shape — `name: obsidian`, `mcpServerConfig.args` of
      `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/literal/inline/path"]`, no `${...}`
      placeholders anywhere in args.

      Assertion: after `runSync`, `.mcp.json`'s `mcpServers.brain.args`
      equals `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/literal/inline/path"]`
      byte-for-byte. No substitution happened because no placeholder
      was present. This locks in the spec's `outcomes.desired[1]`
      ("`interpolateArgs` is a no-op for the default scaffold").

- [x] (Optional, only if the test author wants explicit coverage of
      the advanced-user opt-in surface): add a SEPARATE test that
      writes a brain.aide whose `mcpServerConfig.args` contains a
      `${name}` placeholder, and assert sync writes the literal
      `name` value (e.g. `"obsidian"`) into the corresponding args
      slot. This is not strictly required — the parseBrainAide
      module's tests already cover `interpolateArgs` against
      arbitrary frontmatter fields — but it documents at the sync
      level that the advanced-user surface still works end-to-end.

      If added, name the test: "advanced-user `${name}` placeholder
      resolves at sync time."

### 4. Verify legacy `obsidian` migration is unaffected

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] The legacy `obsidian` key migration test (3c) is unchanged in
      intent — sync still removes `mcpServers.obsidian` when present,
      writes `mcpServers.brain` from the parsed `brain.aide`, and the
      `Removed legacy \`obsidian\` MCP key (migrated to \`brain\`)`
      stdout line still fires only when a deletion actually
      occurred. Per the spec's `outcomes.desired[4]`, the migration
      contract has not moved.

      This step is a READ-and-VERIFY pass: open test 3c, confirm its
      pre-write of `mcpServers.obsidian` (the entry shape — `command`
      and `args` — is incidental; sync writes a fresh `brain` entry
      regardless of what was in `obsidian`), confirm the post-sync
      assertions still apply (`mcpServers.brain` matches
      `EXPECTED_BRAIN_ENTRY`, `mcpServers.obsidian` is absent, output
      mentions migration). The only edit needed in this test is the
      shared updates from step 2: the new path for `writeBrainAide`
      and the new `EXPECTED_BRAIN_ENTRY` shape.

      The `obsidian` entry the test pre-writes can stay as
      `{ command: "npx", args: ["-y", "obsidian-mcp", "/old/vault"] }`
      — its bytes are irrelevant since sync deletes it. Or update to
      `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` for cosmetic consistency; behavior is
      identical either way.

### 5. Final verification

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] 5a. Run the type-checker:

      ```
      npx tsc --noEmit
      ```

      Expected: zero errors. The collapsed `BrainAideConfig` (Plan 2
      output) drops `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `connector`, `tools`;
      this plan's edits should not produce any reads of those fields
      from `result.config`. If tsc reports a missing-field error
      against `result.config.<deprecated>`, the offending reference
      is the bug — remove it.

- [x] 5b. Run the sync verb's tests:

      ```
      npm test -- cli/sync
      ```

      Expected: every test passes. Failures fall into three
      categories that map back to the steps above:

      - "ENOENT: .aide/brain.aide" → step 2's `writeBrainAide` path
        update was missed in some test.
      - "malformed-frontmatter: deprecated fields present:
        connector, rootPath, ..." → step 2's fixture rewrite was
        incomplete; some test still pre-writes the legacy schema.
      - "expected `.aide/config/brain.aide`, got `.aide/brain.aide`"
        in stderr substring assertions → step 1's string edits
        missed a stderr line OR a test still expects the old path
        in its assertion. Either fix the runSync string OR update
        the test's substring.

- [x] 5c. (Defensive) Run the full project test suite to verify the
      sync-module changes haven't tripped a sibling consumer:

      ```
      npm test
      ```

      The shared `writeMcpEntry` helper, the cold-start CLI, and
      `provisionBrain` should all stay green. If any of them fail,
      the failure traces back to a downstream dependency this plan
      didn't change — escalate rather than patch in this module.

## Decisions

- **Edit-not-rewrite scope.** The original `plan.aide` shipped this
  module greenfield, including the shared helper extraction. Both
  shipped — the module exists at `src/cli/sync/`, the shared helper
  exists at `src/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry/`, and the cold-start CLI
  has been refactored to consume the shared helper. This plan is the
  RE-ALIGNMENT pass for the path move + schema collapse. Treating it
  as an edit (rather than a rewrite) keeps the surface area small
  and the diff reviewable. The contract — visible-command-boundary,
  idempotency, legacy-obsidian deletion, exit codes, no-flags — is
  unchanged in shape; only the path strings and the test fixtures
  shift.
- **Shared `writeMcpEntry` is untouched.** The helper takes an
  entries map and a `projectRoot`, and writes whichever keys the
  caller names. It does not know about brain.aide, parseBrainAide,
  or the schema collapse. Editing it would be scope creep. Sync
  consumes its existing API — `entries["brain"] = entry` and
  `entries["obsidian"] = "delete"` — exactly as today.
- **`parseBrainAide` is the load-bearing dependency.** This plan
  cannot land before the parseBrainAide refactor: the new path
  (`.aide/config/brain.aide`) lives in the parser, the deprecated-
  field rejection lives in the parser, and the collapsed
  `BrainAideConfig` type lives in `src/types/index.ts` (collapsed by
  the parseBrainAide plan). Sync consumes those changes; sync does
  not produce them. The Prerequisites section names this dependency
  explicitly so the orchestrator runs the parseBrainAide plan first.
- **Test 3j (interpolation) is replaced, not deleted.** The original
  test exercised `${rootPath}` substitution into args. With
  `rootPath` removed from the schema, that exact assertion is no
  longer expressible. Replacing it with the default-scaffold no-op
  assertion keeps test coverage of the same code path
  (`interpolateArgs` is called from sync's pipeline) but reframes
  the assertion to reflect the new contract: under the default
  scaffold, the helper is a no-op and args pass through verbatim.
  An optional advanced-user-opt-in test for `${name}` is offered
  but not required — `parseBrainAide`'s tests already cover the
  interpolation engine; sync's tests need only verify sync calls
  it (via the no-op pass-through assertion).
- **Error message wording is preserved verbatim where possible.**
  The spec's Good-examples block names exact stderr strings ("No
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` found. Run `npx aidemd-mcp init` ..."
  etc.). Step 1's string edits adjust ONLY the path token; the
  surrounding prose is unchanged. The user-visible terminal
  surface is a contract — even small wording shifts force the
  user (and any documentation that quotes them) to re-learn the
  vocabulary.
- **Malformed-frontmatter `reason` is passed through verbatim.**
  Per the spec's Strategy paragraph "When the parser's reason
  names a deprecated field (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
  `tools`), sync passes the parser's reason through verbatim — the
  user reads exactly which stale fields to remove." Sync wraps the
  reason in surrounding action-naming prose (the path, the "Fix
  the YAML and re-run sync" suffix) but does not paraphrase the
  reason itself. The current code already does this via template
  interpolation (`${result.reason}`); step 1's verification pass
  confirms it stays.
- **Legacy `obsidian` deletion stays unconditional.** Sync always
  passes `obsidian: "delete"` in the entries map. The shared
  helper treats "delete a key that isn't present" as a no-op.
  This keeps a branch out of sync's logic and matches the spec's
  `outcomes.desired[4]` ("the second sync invocation finds
  `obsidian` already gone and `brain` already correct, so it's a
  no-op"). The migration message ("Removed legacy `obsidian` ...")
  fires only when the helper reports `deleted.includes("obsidian")`,
  so the line is suppressed on idempotent re-runs and on cold
  installs that never had `obsidian` in the first place.


